---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---
<Layout
  title="Hanfu in Beijing: Rental, Palace Photos & Where to Wear It"
  description="Hanfu rental in Beijing: prices, where to rent near the Forbidden City, the best photoshoot spots (palaces, Summer Palace, snowy courtyards), and practical tips."
  canonicalUrl="https://www.dresses-hanfu.com/travel/beijing/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Travel", url: "/travel" }, { name: "Beijing", url: "/travel/beijing" }]}
  articleSchema={{
    datePublished: "2026-08-01",
    dateModified: "2026-08-08",
    author: { "@type": "Person", "name": "Hanfu Dress Guide Editorial Team" }
  }}
>
  <SiteHeader active="/travel" />
  <main>
    <article>
      <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/travel/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Travel</a> &rsaquo; Beijing</p>
          <h1>Hanfu in Beijing: Rental, Palace Photos &amp; Where to Wear It</h1>
          <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
            Red walls, gold roofs, a thousand-year-old imperial city. Beijing's Hanfu scene is all about palace grandeur — and in winter, snow against vermilion is one of the most striking photo backdrops in China.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>Quick Facts</h2>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Rental price</strong></td><td>150–350 RMB (~$21–49 USD) for 24h</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Hair &amp; makeup</strong></td><td>Usually included</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Deposit</strong></td><td>300–500 RMB</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Rental period</strong></td><td>Full day (24h) standard; half-day often available</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Peak season</strong></td><td>Autumn (Oct–Nov) and snow days in winter; spring weekends</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>Where to Rent</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The biggest cluster is <strong>outside the Forbidden City's west gate (Jingshan West Street) and near Tiananmen</strong>. Rent here, walk through the palace, and return on your way out. A second cluster sits around the Summer Palace's East Gate.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Beijing pricing runs slightly higher than Xi'an because of palace-demand and higher-quality fabric stock. Ming-dynasty styles with bright red and gold accents dominate — they photograph best against palace walls.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>Best Spots for Hanfu Photos</h2>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Forbidden City</strong> — the pinnacle. Red walls, marble bridges, gold-tiled roofs. Mid-morning light from the east is best; book the palace entry ticket 1–2 weeks ahead online.</li>
            <li><strong>Jingshan Park</strong> — the hill behind the Forbidden City. From the top you get the full palace panorama — a rare aerial backdrop in Hanfu.</li>
            <li><strong>Summer Palace</strong> — Long Corridor and the lakefront (Kunming Lake). Softer, more garden-like than the palace; lovely in autumn.</li>
            <li><strong>Temple of Heaven</strong> — the triple-tier blue roof is iconic. Early morning before crowds.</li>
            <li><strong>Snow days (winter)</strong> — the Forbidden City's red walls against fresh snow is the single most requested Hanfu shot in Beijing. Watch the forecast and book rental the night before.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>What It Actually Costs</h2>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Typical Range</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Hanfu rental (24h)</td><td>150–350 RMB (~$21–49)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Hairstyling</td><td>Usually included</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Photographer (2h)</td><td>400–800 RMB (~$55–110)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Forbidden City ticket</td><td>60 RMB (~$8), reserve online in advance</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Jingshan Park</td><td>10 RMB (~$1.5)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Summer Palace</td><td>30–60 RMB depending on season</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:0.75rem;">A full palace day with rental, styling, and a 2-hour photographer: roughly 900–1,600 RMB (~$125–225 USD).</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>When to Go</h2>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">The best windows</h3>
          <p class="text-muted"><strong>Autumn (October–November)</strong> is the classic choice: crisp air, golden ginkgo, and t-shirt-and-Hanfu weather. <strong>Winter snow</strong> is the special one — red walls, white snow, and near-empty courtyards transform the Forbidden City into the most dramatic Hanfu backdrop in China. Rentals stay open all winter.</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Timing tips</h3>
          <p class="text-muted"><strong>Spring (April–May)</strong> is pleasant but busy with domestic tour groups; book palace tickets a week ahead. <strong>Summer</strong> is hot and humid, but very early mornings still work well. If snow is on the forecast, book rental the night before — the demand spike on snow days is real.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>Signature Experience</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">Time the snow. Beijing's most famous Hanfu photograph is simple: a figure in Ming-style red-and-gold robes standing in a Forbidden City courtyard while snow falls against vermilion walls. Forecast days are announced a few days out. Book your rental the night before, get to the palace for the 8:30 first entry, and shoot before the snow gets trodden into slush — you'll have the courtyard largely to yourself for the first 30 minutes.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>Practical Notes</h2>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Book palace tickets early</strong> — the Forbidden City caps daily entry and often sells out 7+ days ahead in peak season.</li>
            <li><strong>Passport</strong> — required both for rental deposits and palace entry.</li>
            <li><strong>Arrive before the rental shop opens</strong> — palace gates open at 8:30, and the quietest hour is the first one.</li>
            <li><strong>Return time</strong> — most rentals are 24 hours from pickup; late returns cost a half-day fee.</li>
            <li><strong>Language</strong> — palace-area shops usually have English speakers.</li>
            <li><strong>Collar rule</strong> — cross right over left. See <a href="/guides/how-to-wear/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">How to Wear Hanfu</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Winter layering</strong> — ask for a 斗篷 (cape) with a winter rental; it sits over the robe and reads beautifully in palace photos while keeping you warm.</li>
          </ul>

          <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:2rem;background:var(--color-accent-light);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
            <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;font-family:var(--font-display);">Next Steps</p>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem;">
              <a href="/travel/" class="btn btn-outline">All City Guides</a>
              <a href="/guides/how-to-wear/" class="btn btn-outline">How to Wear Hanfu</a>
              <a href="/travel/luoyang/" class="btn btn-outline">Luoyang Guide</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
